A||||5||theogre||Camber/Caster parts for front end||||3||
Z||000000||theogre||11-02-2000||07:45 PM||||There have been a number of questions about camber/caster correction parts for the front of Fiero. Last I saw, the general responce was there are no such parts.<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Well guess what.....   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>There are parts to correct both. The following parts are made under the Moog brand and are available on Carparts.com I'm not sure if the part #'s are carparts.com's or Moog's numbers. These are for 84-87<P>F UPPER CONTROL ARM BS, Problem Solver Allows Additional Camber Adj K6250<BR> <BR>F UPPER CTRL SHAFT ASS, Problem Solver Kit W/Caster Shims, F Suspension; Problem Solver Kit W/Caster Shims  K6357 <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-02-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000001||Bob Englert||11-02-2000||08:36 PM||||Ogre:<P>The Moog part numbers you listed above are mutually exclusive, you can pick one or the other.  There is another option for the 84-87 cars.  Moog also makes an slotted ajustable upper ball joint that will provide greater adjustment for camber for the alignment tech.  FOCOA also sells these at vastly inflated prices.  The P/N is Moog K5292 and is available from Wrenchead.Com for 32.11 each.  I think this would be preferable to the eccentric shaft to provide additional camber adjustment.<P>I have used the replacement upper control arm shaft with caster shims when rebuilding the front end of my daughter's 87 coupe.  This part comes with new rubber upper control arm bushings.  Combine this with the adjustable upper ball joints and the alignment tech should have no excuse for setting toe, camber, and caster within specs!<P><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>87 Coupe<BR>85 SE (For Sale)||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||11-02-2000||10:12 PM||||Re Moog parts:<P>Eccentric upper control arm bushings -- These are designed to give greater positive camber (and who wants that?).  I thought I might be able to use them to relocate the upper control arm pivot downward, but on closer inspection, I don't think they'll have much effect.  If you want a set, I'll sell them cheap.<P>Adjustable upper ball joints -- These work!  I think these are also meant to give greater positive camber; however, they allow some negative adjustment.  Maybe they could be adjusted more negative if you slot the control arm holes.<P>Upper control arm shaft -- Haven't tried this yet.  I am guessing that it's an aftermarket version of GM's shaft kit with 9mm and 3mm caster washers.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bob Englert||11-03-2000||09:26 AM||||The Moog caster adjustment kit is the upper control arm shaft and rubber bushings and a set of thin washers that go between the control arm the the frame mount.  You use 8 washers in any combination (front and back).  This works like the GM 9mm and 3mm washer set but with many more thinner washers to use a more accurate setting is possible in theory.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>87 Coupe<BR>85 SE (For Sale)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||11-03-2000||09:53 AM||||You can get a caster kit for any GM car at the dealer. The sunbird/sunfire/cavaleir/grand am/baretta/corsica/etc kits are the same as the Fiero kits. When I had my car aligned after the suspension rework, (I used the slotted ball joint) The alignment shop had no problems using a generic GM kit to set the caster. Most good alignment shops have the generic kits already so you don't need to buy them.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 11-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||bHooper||11-03-2000||01:14 PM||||i am putting the Moog uper ball joints on my GT, as well as the adjustable cam bolts on the rear.<P>since i am adding poly bushings, i want be able to use the upper caster kit.  although, Bob and i have talked about replacing the existing washers.<P>orief, how much is the kit that you are talking of?<BR><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
